# Commit and PR Guidelines

Keep history clear and PRs reviewable. Use the repo PR template.

## Commit style

- **Preferred:** Conventional commits — `type(scope): message` (e.g. `feat(web): add ImageUpload to convention new`, `fix(convex): require imageUrl on build create`). Types: feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, test, chore.
- **Acceptable:** Short present-tense message (e.g. "Add packing list search", "Fix CORS for preview URLs"). One logical change per commit.
- **Do:** One commit per logical change; message explains what and why when non-obvious.  
**Don't:** Don't commit unrelated changes in one commit; don't use vague messages ("WIP", "fix stuff").

## PR sizing

- **Prefer:** Vertical slices — one feature or fix, testable on its own. Small enough to review in one sitting (e.g. < 400 lines when possible).
- **Do:** Split large work into multiple PRs (e.g. "Add settings routes" then "Add account page content").  
**Don't:** Don't open a single PR with many unrelated features; don't mix refactors with new behavior without clear sections.

## Required checklist (per PR)

Use the checklist in [.github/pull_request_template.md](.github/pull_request_template.md):

- Type of change (bug fix, feature, docs, etc.)
- Service(s) affected (web, mobile, backend, docs, CI)
- Testing: unit/integration/manual as applicable
- Code style, self-review, docs updates, no new warnings
- New/existing tests pass

**Additional:**

- Run **`npm run validate`** (or `npm run ci` / `npm run ci:win`) locally and fix any failures before requesting review.
- If the change touches auth or CORS: confirm trusted origins and allowedOrigins still match (docs/auth.md).
- For new features: add or update implementation docs if needed (see docs/implementation/).

## Screenshots and migration notes

- **UI changes:** Add screenshots (or short video) to the PR so reviewers can see the result.
- **Breaking or config changes:** Note in PR description and in docs (e.g. new env var, Convex schema change). If migration is needed (e.g. data backfill), describe steps.

## Review and definition of done

- **Review:** At least one approval from maintainer; all CI jobs green. Address review comments or document why not.
- **Definition of done:** Feature/fix is implemented; tests added or updated where appropriate; docs updated if behavior or setup changed; local validation passes; no known regressions.

